{"success": true, "name": "puzzles:continual-arc-action-strategy", "file": "references/demo-request-information-gate.md", "content": "# Information gate for costly demo requests

In capped-cost ARC streams, a demo request spends one point just like an incorrect submission. Treat it as an experiment, not as a default continuation.

Before requesting, record internally:
- the unresolved, test-relevant question;
- at least two plausible hypotheses still consistent with all available demos;
- the specific observation in a new pair that would distinguish them;
- why that information is worth the request cost relative to a submission.

If no meaningful competing hypotheses or discriminating observation can be named, do not request yet. Analyze all received pairs first: enumerate changed and unchanged cells/objects, test candidate rules against both, and update the hypothesis ledger. After a demo arrives, perform that delta analysis before any further action; never issue consecutive requests without intervening reasoning. Stop requesting once the new evidence no longer changes the candidate rule or the remaining uncertainty is not test-relevant.

This gate complements the main continual-ARC action procedure; it does not require certainty before requesting, only a concrete, worthwhile experiment.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545531/attempts/bc528d856f1a41f1a28c3fa14e5fbbd6/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/continual-arc-action-strategy/references/demo-request-information-gate.md"}
